# Implementation Plan - Specific Emission Factor Calculator

## Goal Description
Implement a comprehensive Specific Emission Factor Calculator in JavaScript, ported from the provided Python script. This will allow users to calculate custom emission factors based on fuel properties (e.g., carbon content, density, molecular weight) rather than relying solely on standard defaults or manual entry.

## User Review Required
> [!IMPORTANT]
> I will implement this as a strictly client-side library (`ghg-calc-library.js`) first.
> I need to decide on the UI integration. I propose adding a "Calculate" button next to the "Specific Emission Factors" inputs in the [emissions-calculator.html](file:///c:/Users/samsung/Desktop/h/public/emissions-calculator.html) form, which opens a modal/wizard to perform these specific calculations.

## Proposed Changes

### Logic Layer
#### [NEW] [ghg-calc-library.js](file:///c:/Users/samsung/Desktop/h/public/js/ghg-calc-library.js)
- Port `API_GHG_Emission_Factor_Calculator` Python class to JavaScript.
- Implement comprehensive unit conversion to support user inputs in any unit (API, lb/gal, etc.) but OUTPUT to the required standard units:
    - `t/m³`, `kg/m³` (Volume based)
    - `t/GJ`, `kg/kg` (Mass/Energy based)

### UI Layer
#### [MODIFY] [emissions-calculator.html](file:///c:/Users/samsung/Desktop/h/public/emissions-calculator.html)
- Detect when "Use Specific Emission Factors" is checked.
- Dynamically inject/show a "Calculator Panel" *within* the specific factors section (or immediately below) based on the selected Process/Fuel.
    - Example: If Liquid Fuel is selected, show "Density Input" and "Carbon % Input".
    - Example: If Gas Fuel is selected, show "MW Input" and "Carbon % Input".
- Add a "Calculate Factor" button that runs the library function and auto-fills the `spec-co2`, `spec-ch4`, etc., fields.

#### [MODIFY] [emissions-calc.js](file:///c:/Users/samsung/Desktop/h/public/emissions-calc.js)
- Import `ghg-calc-library.js`.
- Listen for changes in Process/Fuel to show/hide the calculator inputs.
- Handle the "Calculate" button click:
    1. Gather inputs (Density, Carbon, etc.).
    2. Call Library function.
    3. Convert result to the selected Unit in the main form (e.g. if user selected `kg/m3`, ensure result is `kg/m3`).
    4. Populate the fields.

## Verification Plan
### Automated Tests
- Create a test script `tests/test-calc-lib.js` execution via Node to verify the JS outputs match the Python script's example outputs.

### Manual Verification
- Open [emissions-calculator.html](file:///c:/Users/samsung/Desktop/h/public/emissions-calculator.html).
- Toggle "Use Specific Emission Factors".
- Click "Calculate".
- Select "Liquid Fuel", enter Density: 32 API, Carbon: 85%.
- Verify result matches Python output (~20-21 lb CO2/gal).
- Click "Apply" and verify the specific factor input is populated.
